  with defaultOn=false in the mirror. No code path turns this on
  implicitly.

I3 baseline intact: TripDetailPage.tsx still imports/renders all
  pre-existing sections (StatCard grid + KVList trip metadata).
  The AI section is added ABOVE the StatCard grid so the baseline
  content remains visible regardless of the gate's state, and the
  manual trip-name field in the KVList remains the canonical surface
  for the deterministic save path. No baseline component was removed,
  reordered destructively, or wrapped in a gate.

I4 zero egress: All provider calls in the new strategy MUST flow
  through `internal/ai/dispatch` which composes the locked decorator
  chain (redaction → rate-limit → cost-cap → audit → trace). Tools
  read trip detail via the existing TripsDetailRepo through a thin
  Source interface — no bespoke SQL, no direct provider import in
  feature code.

I5 hidden UI: AIAutoTripNameSuggestion is wrapped by withAiFeature.
  Both halves of the gate are tested:
    - ai_mode='off' + toggle=true → empty container.
    - ai_mode='cloud' + toggle=false → empty container.
  The positive control (mode='cloud' + toggle=true) proves the gate is
  not permanently hidden by a typo.

I6 404 routes: ai_auto_trip_name_handler_test.go's
  TestAutoTripNamingAIOffHidesSuggestionButton drives the
  guarded route via the real guard.Handler and asserts
  status == http.StatusNotFound when ai_mode='off'.

I7 per-feature opt-in: The toggle-off case in the React test +
  guard's per-feature lookup in the Go handler test prove that mode
  alone is not enough — the feature ID must also be enabled.

I10 type system: Registry entry was added to the canonical Go
  registry.go file; tools/aigen regenerated web/src/ai/features.ts
  from it (verified by --check exit 0 after regeneration). No drift.

I12 client/bg: This slice declares zero background jobs and zero
  push kinds and zero client-storage keys in the registry routes
  metadata (JobNames=[]string{}, PushKinds=[]string{}). The slice
  prompt's "Service worker chunks: ai-auto-trip-naming" and "Client
  storage keys: ai.autoTripNaming.draft" lines in the Off-mode
  contract impact section refer to forward-compat names reserved for
  a future implementation slice — no actual SW chunk or storage code
  is added here, and the AI surface is wrapped with withAiFeature so
  the off-mode behavior is "no component, no draft, no key".

--- Why the chosen design ---

1. tripID as opaque positive-int64 anchor.
   The trip table has a stable primary key (trips.id). The slice's
   REST path /trips/{tripID}/name/draft uses this directly. The
   handler validates tripID > 0 before any tool dispatch — bad IDs
   short-circuit with 400 (HTTP) before the provider is consulted.

2. Propose-only contract — draft_trip_name + validate_trip_name.
   No mutation surface in this slice. The LLM cannot save anything.
   Mirrors the nl-alert-builder pattern: the user reviews the proposed
   name in the UI and uses the canonical (deterministic) save path
   to apply it. validate_trip_name shares a single
   validateTripNameShape() helper with the production
   AITripNameValidator wrapper so a draft accepted by the AI tool is
   byte-equivalent to one a future save handler will accept.

3. Trip source via TripsDetailRepo, not raw SQL.
   AITripSourceAdapter wraps *database.TripsDetailRepo.GetTrip(tripID)
   because TripRepo has no GetByID method. The adapter projects
   *database.TripDetail onto *models.Trip and returns (nil, nil) on
   ErrTripNotFound so the tool can surface "trip not found" without
   bubbling a Go error. No bespoke SQL.

4. Strategy is system-prompt-driven + tool-bound.
   The strategy file pins the FeatureID + system prompt + allowed
   tool list (draft_trip_name, validate_trip_name). Decorator wiring
   is implicit via internal/ai/dispatch. No provider adapter is
   imported here. The system prompt pins:
     - "TeslaSync trip-name assistant"
     - "NEVER save anything"
     - "ALWAYS call draft_trip_name FIRST"
     - "validate_trip_name"
     - "Do NOT invent facts"
     - "Refuse politely"
     - "never quote precise route coordinates"

5. Eval coverage = 3 deterministic goldens.
   weekend_road_trip (positive, multi-drive weekend trip);
   monthly_summary (positive, longer time window);
   refusal_other_trip (refusal — cross-tenant data smuggling).
   Each has a canned mock-provider response wired through F6 so
   `make ai-eval-fast` / `ai-eval -feature auto-trip-naming`
   runs deterministically.

6. Redaction policy = ClassVehicleName only.
   The new PolicyAutoTripNaming allows the user's car name through
   redaction (so the LLM can address it naturally) but keeps every
   place, address, and coordinate tagged. The auto-trip-name strategy
   doesn't need round-trip restoration of redacted tags — the proposed
   name is meant to be human-readable, but tagged place tokens like
   <PLACE:home> remain as identifiers the user can swap manually.

